From: Monica Patel <monica.patel@redwoodinference.com>
To: Samir Shah <samir@keystone.ai>, Priya Rao <priya@keystone.ai>
Cc: Ava Chen <ava.chen@redwoodinference.com>, Vivek Kulkarni <vivek.kulkarni@redwoodinference.com>
Date: 2026-08-23T08:30:00-07:00
Subject: Re: Integration telemetry + graceful fallback plan for Keystone

Samir —

Answers inline and a compact checklist you can hand to your backend team: 

1) 429 vs 503 behavior
- SDK: 429 (RateLimit) -> expose Retry-After header if present, increment local quota counter, apply token-bucket pause. 503 (Unavailable) -> treated as transient; automatic retry with exponential backoff up to 5 attempts. Both events emitted to telemetry with fields: error.code, retry.after, transient_bool.

2) Session affinity during migration
- No opaque cookie rewrite is performed by default. If you need sticky sessions for a specific model variant, we recommend routing key via a custom request header (x-rw-session-id) — SDK will preserve if provided. Weighted shift does not alter client-set headers.

3) Audit logs and request ids
- Telemetry schema includes: request_id, model_variant, container_id (when available), user_id (if provided), and full latency_histogram. Audit export (S3/Customer KMS) includes request_id + model_variant for every completed request.

Implementation checklist (recommended minimal):
- Install Redwood SDK v2.1.x
- Enable telemetry: REDWOOD_TELEMETRY=1 and set REDWOOD_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT to your collector
- Turn on local rate limiter: default token bucket (200r/s burst 400) — tune to your request pattern
- Configure retries: idempotent=true => 3 linear-jitter retries; generate.stream => exponential backoff up to 5
- Endpoint migration plan: start at 10% traffic for 4h, validate traces/error-rate, then 30% for 8h, then 60% for 12–24h. Rollback threshold: +2.5% error delta or +15% latency p50 vs baseline

Sample SDK snippet (Node.js) — server-side handler + retry hook:

const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, telemetry: true });

async function callModel(payload) {
  try {
    return await client.generate(payload, {
      timeout: 120000, // ms
      retries: { maxAttempts: 5, strategy: 'exponential', jitter: true },
      onRetry: (err, attempt) => {
        // emit telemetry event request.retry
        telemetry.emit('request.retry', { attempt, error: err.code, request_id: err.requestId });
      }
    });
  } catch (err) {
    // 429 handling at application layer
    if (err.code === 'RateLimit') {
      // backoff and re-enqueue request with backpressure token
      await backoffSleep(err.retryAfter || 1000);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}

Notes: the sample patch attached contains a Python equivalent and a library helper to map 429->throttling telemetry events.
